"""Beginner-first teaching sequence; small practice examples, never assessment answers."""
COHORTS={
'CML3112': {'programmes':'Civil Engineering and Architecture','object':'building','unit':'degrees C','context':'A building team records indoor temperature to investigate comfort. Civil students can discuss construction and inspection; architecture students can discuss shading and ventilation. Explain the quantities before using them.','formula':'A rectangular floor is 5 m long and 3 m wide. Area = length × width = 15 m².','code':'length_m = 5\nwidth_m = 3\narea_m2 = length_m * width_m\nprint(area_m2)','output':'15','change':'Change length_m to 7. Predict the area before running.','answer':'21 m²','file':'site_inspection_log.csv','field':'crack_width_mm'},
'CML3113': {'programmes':'Mining and Petroleum Engineering','object':'pump','unit':'degrees C','context':'A pump team records temperature to investigate operation. Mining students can relate this to a dewatering pump; petroleum students to a transfer pump. These are teaching examples, not operating limits.','formula':'A pump moves 18 litres in 3 seconds. Flow = volume ÷ time = 6 litres per second.','code':'volume_l = 18\ntime_s = 3\nflow_l_s = volume_l / time_s\nprint(flow_l_s)','output':'6.0','change':'Change volume_l to 24. Predict the flow before running.','answer':'8 litres per second','file':'drill_shift_log.csv','field':'depth_m'},
'CML3111': {'programmes':'Communications, Electrical, Mechatronics and Robotics Engineering','object':'device','unit':'degrees C','context':'A team records device temperature to investigate cooling. Use a communications cabinet, an electrical enclosure or a robot controller as the physical example. No specialist hardware knowledge is assumed.','formula':'A DC test circuit has 12 volts and 2 amperes. Power = voltage × current = 24 watts. This example assumes DC; AC real power needs additional information.','code':'voltage_v = 12\ncurrent_a = 2\npower_w = voltage_v * current_a\nprint(power_w)','output':'24','change':'Change current_a to 3. Predict the power before running.','answer':'36 watts','file':'motor_test_bench.csv','field':'current_a'}
}
# Every session has a retrieval question, explicit vocabulary, one worked example,
# an analogous attempt, and a bridge to the actual public assessment contract.
WEEKS={
2:dict(title='Running and saving your first notebook',recall='What is a file? Point to the downloaded ZIP on your computer. Can you extract it into a folder?',
terms=[('File','A saved item with a name. Its extension tells us the kind: .ipynb is a notebook, .csv a table, .py a Python script and .md a text document.'),('Folder','A container that helps you keep related files together. Extracting a ZIP restores the files inside it.'),('Python and code','Python is the language. Code is the instructions written in that language. Running asks the computer to carry them out.'),('Cell and output','A cell is one notebook block. A code cell runs instructions; a text cell explains them. Output is the result shown below a code cell.'),('Runtime','The running Python session and its current memory. Restarting clears variables. Downloading the notebook saves the document, not the whole running computer.'),('Repository and commit','A repository stores a project and its recorded changes. A commit records a change with a message. Git tracks history; GitHub hosts repositories online.')],
explain=['Open the extracted proof_of_life.ipynb in Colab. Show the code cell, text cell, run button and output with the pointer before asking students to click.', 'Read each symbol aloud: = stores a value under a name; + adds; print(...) displays its input; parentheses hold the input to print. A # starts a comment for a person.', 'Run the following cells separately. After the first cell, readings refers to 12. The second reads the current value, adds 5, then stores the answer under the same name.'],
code='readings = 12\n# Run the next two lines in a SECOND cell.\nreadings = readings + 5\nprint(readings)',out='17 on the first run of the second cell; 22 on its second run; 27 on its third.',
try_it='Restart, run the first cell once, and run the second cell twice. Explain the final number to a partner.',answer='22. Running the entire example from the top resets readings to 12, so a complete run ends at 17 every time.',
bridge='Use proof_of_life.ipynb for A2. Upload the cohort CSV into the runtime Files area and show the filename before reading it. Model repair 01, guide 02–03 and trace 04. Submit the completed main notebook and your own README.md together, plus the repository URL. Repairs 01–04 and hello_engineering.py are practice, not extra submissions.',
core='Each learner runs, explains, restarts, downloads and finds their own main notebook. Create one repository and one useful README using the browser.',stretch='Terminal commands, staging, branching, merging, GitHub Desktop installation and f-string formatting are reference topics. Do not make them prerequisites for A2.',
gate='Ask everyone to point to the output, rerun one cell, and find the saved .ipynb on their own computer. If they cannot, repeat these actions before GitHub.',video='git',next='Week 3 uses the same Colab notebook, but we write small calculations and reusable functions.'),
3:dict(title='Python calculations and reusable functions',recall='Run print(2 + 3). Which part is the instruction, and which part is the output?',
terms=[('Variable','A name referring to a value. In length_m = 5, the right side is evaluated and assigned to length_m.'),('int, float, str, bool','int represents whole numbers; float represents approximate real numbers such as 2.5; str is text in quotes; bool is True or False.'),('Operator','A symbol for an operation: + adds, - subtracts, * multiplies, / divides, ** raises to a power. // floors a quotient, so -5 // 2 is -3.'),('Function','A named, reusable calculation or procedure. def introduces its definition. Parameters are the names for its inputs; arguments are the values supplied when calling it.'),('Indentation and return','Spaces at the start of a line group the function body. return hands a result to the calling code. print displays a value and returns None.'),('Condition','A True/False question. == compares equality; = assigns. if selects a branch; else handles the other case. A colon starts an indented block.')],
explain=['Work the cohort formula by hand before opening Python. State every unit. Use the four-line cohort calculation first; do not begin with formatting, imports or a long function.', 'Introduce one reusable calculation using the example below. Read def, the function name, parentheses, parameter, colon, indentation and return separately. Defining the function does not run its body.', 'Calling double_value(4) supplies 4 for x, calculates 8 and returns 8. The outer print displays it. Then teach an if/else guard with a separate small example.'],
code='def double_value(x):\n    return x * 2\n\nprint(double_value(4))\n\ndef safe_share(total, people):\n    if people == 0:\n        return None\n    return total / people\n\nprint(safe_share(12, 3))',out='8\n4.0',try_it='Write triple_value(x). Predict triple_value(5). Then predict safe_share(12, 0).',answer='triple_value returns x * 3. The calls give 15 and None respectively. The guard returns before division when people is zero.',
bridge='A3 has five named tasks, not five additional projects. Read each public prompt and its exact output name. Signed percentage error = 100 × (measured − expected) / expected. Use a nonzero expected value. For the cohort task: resistive loss = current² × resistance; slab mass = area × thickness × 2400; hydrostatic pressure in kPa = density × 9.81 × depth / 1000. The brief determines which formula you implement. q4 specifies the zero-input behaviour. q5 uses the supplied six readings; trace them on paper, and use your own percentage-error function. Loops are not required yet.',
core='One arithmetic cell, one reusable function and one guarded division explained and modified by each learner; then supported attempts at all five A3 prompts.',stretch='Five-function libraries, module imports, scope details, debugger setup and six independent bug hunts can wait. Demonstrate one ordinary traceback and one unit mistake now.',
gate='Students explain the difference between defining and calling a function, and between return and print. Re-model with double_value if these are confused.',video='numbers',next='Week 4 repeats a calculation for many values using lists and loops.'),
4:dict(title='Lists loops and reading a table',recall='What does a function return? What does print show? Calculate the mean of 2, 4 and 6 by hand.',
terms=[('List and index','A list holds values in order: [2, 4, 6]. Square brackets select a position. readings[0] selects the first item, 2.'),('Loop and iteration','A for loop runs its indented body once per item. Each pass is one iteration.'),('Accumulator','A running total. Set it to zero before the loop, add inside the loop, and read the final total afterward.'),('Dictionary','A collection of named values such as {"temperature_c": 25}. Access the value with row["temperature_c"].'),('CSV and header','CSV means comma-separated values. One line usually represents one record; a header names the columns. CSV numbers arrive as text and may need float conversion.'),('with and import','import makes library tools available. with open(...) opens a file for a block and closes it afterward. A filename must match an existing file in the working folder.')],
explain=['Draw three boxes labelled 0, 1 and 2 holding 2, 4 and 6. Read the first item aloud, then trace the loop by hand.', 'Make a table with columns reading and total: (2,2), (4,6), (6,12). len(readings) counts three items. 12 / 3 is 4.', 'Before a real CSV, show a two-line text extract and name its header. Open the issued CSV together. Explain csv.DictReader as one dictionary per row. Convert only the numeric column and state what should happen to an invalid value.'],
code='readings = [2, 4, 6]\ntotal = 0\nfor reading in readings:\n    total = total + reading\nmean = total / len(readings)\nprint(mean)',out='4.0',try_it='Add 8 to the list. Trace the new total and count before running. What happens if total = 0 is moved inside the loop?',answer='Total 20, count 4, mean 5.0. Resetting inside the loop discards previous additions and leaves only the last value in total.',
bridge='Open the issued W04 notebook and its cohort CSV. Compute the requested valid_count, mean_reading, above_limit and group_means using the exact names in the notebook. First identify the correct field and its unit. Keep the CSV beside the notebook locally or upload it into the Colab runtime. The written interpretation is practice.',
core='Trace one loop, read the actual header and implement one summary together before independent attempts. Give prompts and hints during the investigation.',stretch='Nested comprehensions, arbitrary exception handling, a blank-page investigation and data-source hunting are later practice. Never use except: pass to silently erase evidence.',
gate='Students trace the running total without executing and identify the numeric field in the real CSV. Revisit a three-item list if either step fails.',video=None,next='Week 5 uses pandas to perform the same table operations with less code.'),
5:dict(title='Using pandas to answer table questions',recall='Identify one row, one column, and the mean of three readings. Explain the running-total loop.',
terms=[('Library and pandas','A library is reusable code. pandas provides tools for data tables. import pandas as pd makes pd a short name for that library.'),('DataFrame','A table with named columns and indexed rows. df is just the variable name we choose for this table.'),('Series','One labelled column. df["kwh"] selects the column named kwh.'),('Method and attribute','A method performs an operation, as in df.head(). An attribute provides a stored property, as in df.shape. Parentheses call an operation.'),('Mask and filter','A comparison produces True/False for each row. df[mask] keeps the True rows.'),('groupby and aggregation','groupby collects rows with the same label. An aggregation such as sum or mean returns a summary for each group.')],
explain=['Draw a four-row table before running the example. Describe a reading of kWh as energy, and kW as power; the units are different.', 'Explain dictionary braces, column labels in quotes and lists of values before constructing the DataFrame. In class, read the issued campus_energy.csv with pd.read_csv after this tiny example.', 'Run the column selection, comparison and filtered table in separate cells. Then group rows by building and add energy within each group.'],
code='import pandas as pd\ndf = pd.DataFrame({"building": ["A", "A", "B", "B"],\n                   "kwh": [2, 4, 5, 7]})\nprint(df["kwh"].mean())\nprint(df[df["kwh"] > 4])\nprint(df.groupby("building")["kwh"].sum())',out='Mean 4.5. The filter keeps B rows with 5 and 7. Group totals: A = 6, B = 12 kWh.',try_it='Use > 5 instead. Predict the surviving rows. Which building has the largest total?',answer='Only the row with 7 survives. Building B has the larger total, 12 kWh.',
bridge='A5 uses the supplied fictional campus_energy.csv, not a live Kaggle download. Teach read_csv, nunique, division of columns, > filtering, mean and groupby(...).sum().idxmax() before the six public tasks. idxmax returns the label of the largest value. Explain how a rate differs from a total and how a mean of rates differs from a total-cost/total-energy ratio.',
core='Load the issued table and work one selection and grouped total together. Students then attempt the six A5 questions with hints.',stretch='NumPy broadcasting, broad Kaggle searches and twelve-plus-ten independent questions are optional extension. No change of editor is needed.',
gate='Each student predicts a filter and explains what one group total means before running it.',video=None,next='Week 6 checks what happens when the data table contains mistakes or gaps.'),
6:dict(title='Cleaning data and explaining changes',recall='Select a column and filter rows. Explain why a sum and a mean answer different questions.',
terms=[('Raw data','The original supplied records. Keep these unchanged so another person can check your decisions.'),('Missing value','A value that was not recorded, represented here by None in Python records. Missing does not automatically mean zero.'),('Record and equipment ID','A record ID identifies an observation. An equipment ID identifies the source and may repeat legitimately. Remove repeated observations only under the stated rule.'),('Median and imputation','The median is the middle sorted value, or the mean of the two middle values. Imputation fills a missing value using an explicit rule and introduces uncertainty.'),('Audit','A count and explanation of what changed, why it changed and how many records remain.'),('Chart axes','A chart maps values to positions. Read the horizontal variable, vertical variable and their units before interpreting a pattern.')],
explain=['Use the example below to sort four valid readings. The median is (22 + 24) / 2 = 23. Filling a blank with 23 is a stated teaching rule, not a recovered measurement.', 'Model a defect register: observation, evidence, decision, affected count, limitation. Work one repeated record and one missing value together.', 'Draw a scatter plot from four pairs on the board. A pattern can motivate investigation without proving that one variable causes the other.'],
code='from statistics import median\nraw = [20, None, 22, 24, 80]\nknown = []\nfor value in raw:\n    if value is not None:\n        known.append(value)\nfill = median(known)\ncleaned = []\nfor value in raw:\n    if value is None:\n        cleaned.append(fill)\n    else:\n        cleaned.append(value)\nprint(fill)\nprint(raw)\nprint(cleaned)',out='23.0\n[20, None, 22, 24, 80]\n[20, 23.0, 22, 24, 80]',try_it='Explain why 80 was retained. Replace 24 with 26, predict the new fill value, and check that cleaning still leaves raw unchanged.',answer='An unusual value alone does not prove an error. The task did not authorize deleting 80. With 26 replacing 24, the middle known values are 22 and 26, so the new fill is 24. The raw list still contains None.',
bridge='W06 uses a list of record dictionaries. Show one record and one field lookup before the function interfaces. Parse CSV blank numeric cells as None. Model copying with dict(row) and list.append. Walk through the published cleaning order before students write clean_records, summarize_records and audit_cleaning. Teach set membership for duplicate IDs and grouped accumulation. Explain that written evidence belongs in My written evidence, not in code comments.',
core='Trace the cleaning policy on a tiny table, preserve raw input, count changes and explain one limitation. Use a prepared scaffold for the complete published interfaces.',stretch='MCAR/MAR/MNAR names, five chart families and six unaided defect hunts are reference. Teach missingness causes in everyday language first.',
gate='Students distinguish repeated equipment from duplicate records and explain why an outlier is not automatically deleted.',video='boxplot',next='Week 7 assesses the skills already rehearsed. Complete an offline practice run before test day.'),
7:dict(title='Preparing for Practical Test 1',recall='Can you open the provided notebook and data on the prepared lab computer without internet?',terms=[('Offline','The activity runs on the local lab computer. Colab requires internet and is not the test environment.'),('Interface','The exact function name, inputs and returned output expected by the published task.'),('Evidence','The calculations and written reasoning that support your conclusion.'),('Submission packet','All required files saved together with your Student Registration Number in the stated places.')],explain=['Use this lesson as preparation in Week 6 or a scheduled rehearsal, not as new teaching inside the timed test.', 'Demonstrate one unrelated practice table, one function check and saving a notebook plus the typed Part B document. Use a blank practice packet, never a live test variant.', 'Have every learner locate their files after saving. Confirm the published permitted resources and the failure-reporting procedure.'],code='values = [3, 5, 7]\nprint(len(values))\nprint(sum(values) / len(values))',out='3\n5.0',try_it='Write a one-sentence interpretation with a unit and one limitation for three fictional temperature readings.',answer='The mean of these three fictional temperature readings is 5 degrees C; three teaching values cannot establish the operating behaviour of real equipment.',bridge='Use the official TEST1 brief, interfaces and rubric. Keep its 145-minute assessment allocation and collection procedure. Release depends on the separate assessment-readiness checks; this practice material does not authorize release.',core='Offline access, familiar interfaces, data reading, valid summaries, reasoning and saving.',stretch='No new tools, new packages or surprise functions on test day.',gate='Every learner completes the offline save-and-reopen rehearsal. Escalate lab-access failures before the test.',video=None,next='Week 8 introduces a model that learns numerical relationships from examples.'),
8:dict(title='A first prediction model',recall='Given three measured temperatures, calculate the mean. How would you predict a fourth temperature without any other information?',terms=[('Feature and target','Features are inputs available when making a prediction. The target is the quantity we want to predict.'),('Training validation and test','Training fits the model. Validation helps choose it. The final test estimates performance after choices are fixed. Repeated equipment must stay in the same partition when assessing unseen equipment.'),('Baseline','A simple reference prediction. A training-mean baseline predicts the same training mean for every new example.'),('fit and predict','fit learns from training inputs and targets. predict uses the fitted rule on new inputs without receiving their true targets.'),('Linear model and tree','A linear model combines inputs with learned weights. A regression tree asks feature-threshold questions and predicts a value at each leaf.'),('MAE and RMSE','MAE averages absolute prediction errors. RMSE squares errors, averages them and takes the square root. Both use target units. RMSE is more sensitive to large errors.')],explain=['Use the small invented table below solely to see fit and predict. Real training needs more data, representative evaluation and careful partitioning.', 'A list of lists is a table: each inner list holds the features of one observation. y_train holds one target per training row in the same order. Never reorder one without the other.', 'Read imports and model creation separately. The model line creates an unfitted object. fit learns; predict applies. Explain the shallow tree now because the W08 assignment asks for it, then revisit classification trees in Week 9.'],code='from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\nX_train = [[1], [2], [3], [4]]\ny_train = [12, 14, 16, 18]\nnew_inputs = [[2.5]]\n\nmodel = LinearRegression()\nmodel.fit(X_train, y_train)\n\npredictions = model.predict(new_inputs)\nprint(predictions.round(2).tolist())',out='[15.0]',try_it='On paper compare actual [10, 20] with predictions [12, 16]. Find MAE and RMSE. Is MAE a plus/minus guarantee for each prediction?',answer='Absolute errors are 2 and 4: MAE 3. Squared errors 4 and 16: RMSE sqrt(10), about 3.162. MAE is an average, not an individual error bound.',bridge='W08 asks for baseline, linear and depth-4 tree prediction lists plus error metrics. Teach training-only median imputation, Pipeline, list-to-table conversion and dictionary return values before those interfaces. Only load_pct and ambient_c are predictors. Introduce grouped partitions now, not first in Week 10. The written comparison is a supplied scenario; do not pretend to have measured its numbers.',core='Read the small table, fit a baseline and linear model in separate steps, explain one tree prediction and hand-calculate the two error measures.',stretch='Deriving least squares, leaderboard competition, many residual plots and coefficient inference are reference. R² may be negative and is not an accuracy percentage.',gate='Students identify the target, explain why it is absent from prediction inputs and calculate one absolute error.',video='regression',next='Week 9 predicts a category and counts different kinds of mistakes.'),
9:dict(title='Classification and the cost of mistakes',recall='What distinguishes predicting a temperature from predicting warning or no warning?',terms=[('Class and positive label','A class is a category. Here 1 means warning and 0 means no warning. Positive means the named class, not good news.'),('TP and TN','True positive is a correctly flagged warning. True negative is a correctly unflagged non-warning.'),('FP and FN','False positive is a false alarm. False negative is a missed warning.'),('Precision','Of all predicted warnings, the fraction that were actual warnings: TP / (TP + FP).'),('Recall','Of all actual warnings, the fraction detected: TP / (TP + FN).'),('Threshold and cost','A threshold converts a score to a decision. A cost rule states how much each type of mistake matters. Lower thresholds usually increase recall; precision need not decrease at every step.')],explain=['Place four paper observations in a table of actual label and predicted label. Classify each before showing the four-cell matrix. State which axis is actual and which is predicted.', 'Use counts before fractions. The denominator of precision is predicted positives; the denominator of recall is actual positives. Explain what happens if either denominator is zero.', 'Only after the count exercise, show a classifier fit/predict demonstration. Logistic regression predicts a category despite its name. Probability estimates need not be well calibrated.'],code='actual = [1, 1, 0, 0]\npredicted = [1, 0, 1, 0]\nfor truth, guess in zip(actual, predicted):\n    print(truth, guess)\n# Count the four categories on paper before calculating.\ntp, fn, fp, tn = 1, 1, 1, 1\nprint(tp / (tp + fp))\nprint(tp / (tp + fn))\nprint(5 * fn + fp)',out='Four paired rows, then precision 0.5, recall 0.5 and teaching cost 6.',try_it='Change the second prediction to 1. Count again. Does this improve both recall and the assumed cost?',answer='TP=2, FN=0, FP=1, TN=1. Precision 2/3, recall 1, cost 1. This particular change improves recall and cost.',bridge='Implement classification_metrics on arbitrary paired binary labels, not only the example. Use the published zero-denominator convention. Read the supplied W09 comparison separately. Teacher-written cost assumptions are not approved operating limits. All claimed benefits require real-world evaluation.',core='Correctly label four outcomes, calculate precision/recall/cost and explain a missed-warning trade-off.',stretch='Three independently built classifiers, ROC/AUC and full threshold curves are extension after the basic matrix is secure.',gate='Ask students to explain a false negative in their own programme and identify the denominator of recall.',video='confusion',next='Week 10 asks whether a good-looking validation result can be trusted.'),
10:dict(title='Honest validation and leakage',recall='Which rows fit the model? Which rows help choose it? Which rows must remain untouched until the end?',terms=[('Generalisation','Performance on new cases from the intended setting, not merely on records already used.'),('Overfitting and underfitting','Overfitting captures training-specific detail that fails to transfer. Underfitting misses useful patterns. Score gaps are clues, not a complete diagnosis.'),('Leakage','Information enters model development that would not legitimately be available, such as the answer or information from evaluation rows.'),('Group split','Keep all observations from the same equipment in one partition when the goal is prediction for unseen equipment.'),('Preprocessing and pipeline','Preprocessing changes inputs, for example filling missing values. A pipeline fits these steps on training data and applies the same fitted changes to new inputs.'),('Cross-validation','Repeat fitting and validation on several partitions of the training/development data. Respect groups or time order. Keep the final test outside tuning.')],explain=['Write equipment letters on paper cards and distribute whole groups to train, validation and test. Explain why two observations of the same machine can share information.', 'Fit a missing-value replacement on training values [20, 22, 24]: the median is 22. A new hotter observation must not change that fitted median.', 'Show a counterexample: if every input contains a field called final_inspection_result recorded after the outcome, an excellent score can be meaningless for earlier prediction.'],code='records = [{"id": 1, "equipment": "A"},\n           {"id": 2, "equipment": "A"},\n           {"id": 3, "equipment": "B"}]\ntrain = []\nvalidation = []\nfor row in records:\n    if row["equipment"] == "A":\n        train.append(row["id"])\n    else:\n        validation.append(row["id"])\nprint(train, validation)',out='[1, 2] [3]',try_it='A second observation from B arrives. Where does it go? Why not put it into training just to balance row counts?',answer='It stays with B in validation. Mixing a source across partitions can give an overly optimistic estimate for unseen equipment.',bridge='W10 specifies E1–E4 train, E5 validation, E6 test and preserves input order. Use the assigned equipment_id field and record_id outputs. Reuse the W08 fitting pattern. Explain that GroupShuffleSplit and time-based splitting solve different deployment questions; use the rule in the issued task.',core='Trace whole-group membership, identify one future-information leak and explain training-only preprocessing.',stretch='Multiple adversarial notebooks, elaborate validation curves and several cross-validation variants are extensions after the core split is understood.',gate='Students explain why E6 cannot be used to choose a feature or tune the tree. If they cannot, return to the paper partitions.',video='validation',next='Week 11 adds one feature and checks whether it helps under the same protocol.'),
11:dict(title='Creating and testing a useful feature',recall='What makes an input available at prediction time? Why keep validation membership fixed?',terms=[('Derived feature','A new input calculated from existing inputs. Its definition and unit must be stated.'),('Ratio and interaction','A ratio divides quantities; an interaction multiplies them. Both need a domain explanation and a unit check.'),('Encoding','Representing a category numerically. One-hot uses separate indicator columns. Arbitrary integer labels can falsely suggest an order.'),('Scaling','Changing the numerical scale of features. Fit learned scaling on training data only. It matters for distance-based models but is not required by ordinary decision trees.'),('Ablation','Compare a model with and without a feature while keeping the data partitions, model family and metric fixed.'),('Improvement','For an error metric, baseline error minus new error. A negative result means the added feature worsened validation error in that comparison.')],explain=['Begin with a percentage: 40% means 40 / 100 = 0.4. Converting percentage to fraction changes units but adds no new information.', 'An interaction may help a simple model express a relationship, but usefulness is tested rather than assumed. An interaction involving Celsius is an empirical feature, not automatically a physical law.', 'Read a two-row results table: base MAE 3, extended MAE 2.5 gives improvement 0.5 target units. Ask which other experimental choices must stay fixed.'],code='load_pct = 40\nambient_c = 25\nload_fraction = load_pct / 100\ninteraction = load_pct * ambient_c\nprint(load_fraction, interaction)\nbase_mae = 3.0\nextended_mae = 2.5\nprint(base_mae - extended_mae)',out='0.4 1000\n0.5',try_it='If the extended error becomes 3.4, calculate improvement and decide whether this evidence supports retaining the feature.',answer='Improvement is -0.4. This validation comparison does not support retaining it. One split is limited evidence; the final test remains untouched.',bridge='W11 names four output features and a two-model ablation. Explain output order, preservation of input rows, full precision and training-only fitting. Use the supplied written scenario for the interpretation requested there. Do not substitute this practice example’s result.',core='Create one feature, name its unit and interpret one controlled comparison.',stretch='Five independent features, a full ColumnTransformer construction and domain-code features are extension. Never invent engineering limits.',gate='Students explain why the largest-looking feature or the most complex model need not improve predictions.',video=None,next='Week 12 chooses a model under error, speed and explanation requirements.'),
12:dict(title='Choosing and explaining a model',recall='Which metric is smaller-is-better? Why compare on the same validation rows?',terms=[('Constraint','A condition a candidate must satisfy, such as MAE at most 3 and latency at most 10 ms.'),('Latency','Time taken to produce a prediction under stated measurement conditions. Hardware and measurement method matter.'),('Tie-break','A published rule for choosing between candidates that tie on the main criterion.'),('Hyperparameter','A setting chosen before fitting, such as tree depth. Tune using validation or cross-validation, not the final test.'),('Local explanation','Why a model made one particular prediction. This is different from global importance, which summarises behaviour over many cases.'),('Conditions of use','The cases and operating circumstances for which the available evidence supports considering the model.')],explain=['Cross out ineligible candidates before ranking errors. Then apply the tie-break rule, rather than choosing the most sophisticated model.', 'Demonstrate dictionary field access and ordered comparisons before asking learners to implement selection. A sorted list key can be a tuple: first metric, then speed, then name.', 'Write a short recommendation: question, fair comparison, chosen candidate, reason, limitation and what new evidence could change the choice.'],code='candidates = [{"name": "simple", "error": 3, "ms": 2},\n              {"name": "complex", "error": 1, "ms": 20}]\neligible = []\nfor item in candidates:\n    if item["error"] <= 3 and item["ms"] <= 10:\n        eligible.append(item)\nprint([item["name"] for item in eligible])',out="['simple']",try_it='A third model has error 3 and latency 1 ms. Under lowest error then lowest latency, which eligible model wins?',answer='The third model. Both eligible models have error 3, but 1 ms is faster than 2 ms.',bridge='W12 uses fields name, validation_mae and latency_ms. Meet both inclusive constraints, then rank by error, latency and alphabetical name. Return None if no candidate qualifies. Complete the error function and interpret the separately supplied scenario. Keep project comparisons and graded interfaces distinct.',core='Apply eligibility and tie-break rules by hand, implement them, and justify a recommendation with a limitation.',stretch='A large grid search, four new model implementations and an adversarial defence are optional practice. They must not crowd out the core decision process.',gate='Students justify a valid simple model over a more accurate but ineligible one, and distinguish local explanation from global importance.',video='validation',next='Week 13 makes the whole project understandable and runnable by another person.'),
13:dict(title='Making the project understandable and runnable',recall='Can you explain what your project predicts, its unit or class, and the validation rule in one minute?',terms=[('Reproducibility','Another person can use the stated data, environment and steps to check the result. Terminology varies; state the operational test.'),('Dependency','A package your code needs. Record versions actually used, rather than guessing them.'),('Relative path','A location interpreted from the current working folder. It is not always relative to the notebook file itself.'),('README','The project’s instructions: question, files, data source, environment, run order, results and limitations.'),('Peer review','Someone else tests the instructions and reports a specific observation and suggested improvement.'),('Contribution evidence','A record of each person’s work, tied to files, commits or explanations. It does not require making a private repository public.')],explain=['Demonstrate a clean run from a fresh session with the actual files. If a variable exists only from an earlier manual cell run, fix the run order.', 'Explain every dependency and test a relative path. Include data-access instructions if the data cannot be redistributed.', 'Model actionable feedback: “Step 2 names data.csv, but the supplied file is readings.csv; please make them match.”'],code='from pathlib import Path\nprint(Path.cwd().name)\n# The name depends on the folder where Python is running.',out='Your current working folder name; it is environment-dependent.',try_it='Ask a partner to follow your README without verbal help. Record the first step that fails.',answer='Revise that step and repeat. A claim that the notebook runs is supported by a fresh run, not by saved outputs alone.',bridge='No new weekly assignment. Continue the official project deliverables and Week 13 quiz. Rehearse offline TEST2 saving and the published function interfaces before Week 14. Do not add an extra upload.',core='Fresh run, clear README, data provenance, contribution evidence and one useful peer review.',stretch='Package refactoring, complex repository layout and extra tooling are optional. Keep a working simple notebook.',gate='A partner can locate the input data and follow the run order without hints.',video='git',next='Week 14 assesses the modelling and reasoning skills already rehearsed.'),
14:dict(title='Preparing for Practical Test 2',recall='Can you name the target, fit only on training records and explain how you would keep the test untouched?',terms=[('Protocol','The stated procedure for splitting, preprocessing, fitting, choosing and evaluating.'),('Baseline comparison','Evidence that a candidate improves on a simple reference under the same evaluation rules.'),('Typed reasoning','Your own written account of the decision, evidence and limits in the required Part B file.'),('Recovery copy','A recent saved copy that limits lost work if the computer or runtime fails.')],explain=['Use this preparation before the test. Do not consume timed assessment minutes introducing new syntax.', 'Rehearse the actual notebook and typed-document save procedure on the provisioned lab machine using separate practice values.', 'Review one error calculation and one leakage explanation. An unexplained high score is not enough evidence of a useful engineering model.'],code='actual = [4, 8]\npredicted = [5, 6]\nerrors = []\nfor a, p in zip(actual, predicted):\n    errors.append(abs(a - p))\nprint(sum(errors) / len(errors))',out='1.5',try_it='Explain which data may fit the imputer and which may select a model.',answer='Training data fit the imputer; validation/development data support selection. The final test is kept out of both.',bridge='Use TEST2’s official brief, interfaces, time allocation, permitted resources and packet requirements. Confirm calibrated grading, lab readiness and controlled collection before release.',core='Rehearse already taught processes, file saving and technical failure reporting.',stretch='No new libraries, algorithms or environment installation on test day.',gate='Each learner can save and reopen both required file types in the offline setting.',video=None,next='Week 15 presents the project and explains individual contributions.'),
15:dict(title='Presenting the project and individual contribution',recall='What question did your team investigate, and what result can you support with evidence?',terms=[('Finding','A statement supported by a calculation, table or plot.'),('Limitation','A reason to restrict the claim, such as synthetic data, a small sample or a different operating setting.'),('Individual defence','Your own explanation of specific decisions and contributions, using evidence from the project.'),('Reflection','What you learned, what changed in your approach and what you plan to improve next.')],explain=['Model a six-minute presentation: one minute for the problem, one for the data, two for the method and result, one for limitations and one for contribution and next step.', 'Explain each chart’s axes and unit before stating its finding. Use the actual measured validation results and state how they were obtained.', 'Practise saying “I do not know yet” followed by the evidence needed to resolve the question. Do not claim deployment readiness from a teaching dataset.'],code=None,out=None,try_it='Explain one project result to a partner without using the words algorithm, parameter or pipeline. Then introduce each technical term you need.',answer='A clear explanation names what was measured, what was predicted, how wrong the predictions were and what the result cannot establish.',bridge='Use the published project and DEMO requirements. Each individual submits their own typed defence and reflection through the designated route. Live questioning and peer comments support learning; they are not a new manual marking workload.',core='Explain the problem, evidence, limitation and individual contribution accurately.',stretch='New models and a last-minute redesign are unnecessary. Fix broken run instructions first.',gate='Each student can explain one decision they personally worked on and point to its evidence.',video=None,next='Use the reflection to choose specific revision goals before Semester 2.')
}
